        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-25T01:50:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It seems like this makes it harder to evolve the HAL.\n\nSuppose that we later want to extend the number of buckets to 32. If we constrain this type to uint16_t, then extending to 32 buckets would require a major version bump, no?\n\nIf we stick with uint32_t, then we could accommodate 32 buckets with a smaller API change (just changing the MAX_BUCKETS value).\n\nOr maybe I\u0027m misunderstanding the HIDL model?",
      "parentUuid": "64bfd358_3b68854f",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7095e26f_d2e8df76",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074459
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-25T01:52:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah. Actually, I kind of agree. I don\u0027t really see any benefit of making this uint16_t. We any have constants/capabilities which define the max possible number.",
      "parentUuid": "3f49e9e2_9ee9488b",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f769e37_78a5f4db",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-25T01:58:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It wouldn\u0027t require a major version bump, it would require an additional function. What if you need to extend the buckets to 64? Really, shouldn\u0027t this be vec\u003cbool\u003e?",
      "parentUuid": "7095e26f_d2e8df76",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2503e986_3229e665",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074459
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-27T20:04:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The number of scans currently is around 5-6. This is uses as a bitmask in the legacy HAL and in framework as well. I\u0027m not sure we want to make this a vec of booleans just for an interface that might never change.",
      "parentUuid": "6f769e37_78a5f4db",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1fa3b772_9e3263de",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-27T20:04:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "An additional function is certainly better than a major version bump. But it\u0027d be even better if we didn\u0027t need to change the framework side of the code to be aware of the new function.\n\nIs saving 2 bytes important here? As for 64 buckets, I\u0027m fine with making the a uint64_t, to leave more room for expansion.",
      "parentUuid": "6f769e37_78a5f4db",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9153df47_05417c34",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-27T21:21:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "maybe I should have said, what if you had 65 buckets?",
      "parentUuid": "1fa3b772_9e3263de",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "371dcfe4_3215207a",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-27T22:18:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There\u0027s a rationale for sticking to machine words, in that moving beyond that adds complexity.\n\nI don\u0027t see the rationale for choosing a smaller-than-native machine word. The CPU on any Android device supports at-least 32-bits natively. What do we gain by going with something smaller?",
      "parentUuid": "9153df47_05417c34",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "771c1079_9c0bb707",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/IWifiStaIfaceEventCallback.hal",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 39,
      "author": {
        "id": 1120458
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-27T23:59:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "By using uint16_t, you\u0027re no longer passing around 16 bits of always-useless information. This isn\u0027t a performance gain, but it\u0027s a sanity gain. If you pass those extra 16 bits, you need a protocol to handle the extra bits. It\u0027s for sanity",
      "parentUuid": "371dcfe4_3215207a",
      "revId": "50c1f66802f32bc1a3c4a4d1334a578799ab4e30",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
